+        _desc = "Four-way pixel-smooth camera over a META_TILESET room. The room is flattened at compile time into a char plane and a colour plane, so the runtime never decodes stamps. JSR MSC_L/MSC_R/MSC_U/MSC_D to move the camera one pixel that way, and JSR MSC_Update every frame so a coarse step still finishes when nothing is moving. Clicking any of those five names on the node drops a ready-made JSR node for it. Fine scroll is $D016 for L/R and $D011 for U/D. The window is cols 0-38 and rows 0-24 - the outer cells scroll too, because a new column or row has to be loaded into a cell the border is covering or it appears at full width the instant it is written. 38-column mode covers 7 pixels on the left and 9 on the right, 24-row mode 4 top and 4 bottom, so col 38 and row 24 reveal perfectly from 0 to 7 pixels, while col 0 and row 0 leave a single pixel of pop - the left cover is 7 pixels and a character is 8, so that last pixel is a hardware limit; cover it with a sprite if it shows. Col 39 is the only pad and is invisible at every fine-scroll value. BLANK CH is the character the screen is cleared to at init and the one left in col 39; it defaults to char 0 rather than $20, because $20 is a space only in the ROM charset and is usually a real glyph in a custom one. COLOUR MODE decides what happens to colour RAM. FIXED is the stock C64 setting: in multicolour text mode the three shared colours come from $D021/$D022/$D023 and the colour-RAM nibble only supplies the fourth, so a room drawn from one palette needs no colour scrolling at all - the nibble is written once at init, the coarse step costs a single frame and the colour plane is never built. SHIFT C64U scrolls colour RAM per cell for maps that really do vary, doing both passes in the same frame at roughly twice the cost - about 80 percent of a PAL frame, which fits inside a frame at 1 MHz but runs down the visible display, so it is clean on a C64 Ultimate in turbo and tears on stock hardware. There is no longer a two-frame SHIFT: splitting the passes was sequencing rather than cycles, so the one-frame colour lag it caused showed on a fast machine too; projects saved with it load as FIXED. OMIT TOP and OMIT BOTTOM shrink the scroll window by up to 8 rows at each end. Those rows keep the blank char and are never touched again, so they are free space for a HUD, and the coarse step stops copying them. Top rows are worth more than bottom rows: dropping one from the top removes the same work and also gives the raster 8 more lines to travel before it reaches the first row that scrolls, so the copy gets a later deadline as well - roughly 13.9 raster lines of headroom per top row against 5.9 per bottom row, against the 41 or so the top-right tear needs back. Three rows off the top usually clears it on its own, and the node shows the coarse-step cost against the budget in lines, red while it still overruns. Two consequences to plan for: the first row that scrolls is fully visible, so a vertical coarse step pops a whole character at that seam, and $D011 YSCROLL moves the whole screen, so a char-based HUD rides with the vertical fine scroll unless you split the raster over it or draw it with sprites.";
